 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1999, NASA lost a Mars probe to one tiny math mistake.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.685
  },
  {
   "text": "The Mars Climate Orbiter flew nine months to reach Mars.",
   "s": 3.985,
   "e": 6.188
  },
  {
   "text": "Engineers steered it with small thruster burns.",
   "s": 6.487,
   "e": 8.237
  },
  {
   "text": "But one team's software measured force in pounds.",
   "s": 8.537,
   "e": 10.473
  },
  {
   "text": "NASA's navigators assumed newtons, the metric unit.",
   "s": 10.773,
   "e": 12.923
  },
  {
   "text": "Nobody converted, so every push was four times bigger than they thought.",
   "s": 13.223,
   "e": 16.073
  },
  {
   "text": "The orbiter drifted off course.",
   "s": 16.373,
   "e": 17.518
  },
  {
   "text": "It arrived just fifty-seven kilometers above Mars, way too low.",
   "s": 17.818,
   "e": 20.642
  },
  {
   "text": "It hit the atmosphere and was never heard from again.",
   "s": 20.942,
   "e": 22.996
  },
  {
   "text": "The fix: one multiplication. The cost: $125 million.",
   "s": 23.296,
   "e": 26.945
  }
 ],
